us":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2479,2203,2520],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-native-0040","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-note-32","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":40,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"Brunschwig raises the question (pp.279-80) whether Aristotle is considering the case of something that is sleeping","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2527,2190,2568],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-native-0041","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-note-32","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":41,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"eternally, or of something that only sleeps from time to time. in my view, he is only asking whether it is sleeping","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2575,2208,2616],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-native-0042","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-note-32","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":42,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"now; although, of course, the same reasons that show that it is not sleeping now also show that it does not sleep at","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2622,666,2664],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-native-0043","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-note-32","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":43,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"any other time either","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2662,2143,2712],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[{"bbox":null,"end_char":2,"semantic_role":"footnote_marker","span_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-native-0044-visual-qa-marker-00","start_char":0,"styles":["superscript"],"target_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-note-33","text":"33"}],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-native-0044","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-note-33","paragraph_role":"start","reading_order":44,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"33 in case anyone is interested: it's not so easy to say what Endymion's story was--different sources have a wild","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2718,2187,2760],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-native-0045","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":45,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"variety of versions of the myth. he is often said to have come from Thessaly and to be the founder of Elis; on the","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2766,2236,2808],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-native-0046","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":46,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"other hand, sometimes he lives in Caria (in a cave on Mount Latmos)--apparently his resting-place is shown in both","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2814,2222,2856],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-native-0047","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":47,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"places. usually the Moon falls in love with him; but sometimes Sleep does. sometimes the god/goddess, so as to be","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2862,2216,2904],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-native-0048","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":48,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"able to enjoy looking on him always, keeps him always asleep; sometimes, after the god/goddess falls in love with","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2910,2249,2951],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-native-0049","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":49,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"him, Zeus gives him a choice of mortality or immortality, and he chooses to be immortal but always asleep, with the","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[300,2958,2132,2999],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-native-0050","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":50,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"consequence that he will never grow old (thus apparently he has a better fate than Tithonus, also beloved of a","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"},{"bbox":[1250,3073,1300,3110],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"furniture","content_class":"page_number","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"zxx","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925-native-0051","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"not_applicable","reading_order":51,"source":"human_draft","structure_status":"candidate_unverified","text":"917","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"candidate_unverified"}],"page_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf925","schema":"mpdf-closed-world-ocr-gold-page","schema_version":"1.2"}
